  "identity": {
    "playing_style": "Under Kovač Dortmund abandoned their old 'heavy-metal' identity for a structured 3-4-2-1: Anton, Schlotterbeck and Bensebaini in front of Kobel, wing-backs providing the width, Sabitzer/Nmecha (now Jobe Bellingham, Chukwuemeka) in the double pivot and two flexible No. 10s behind Guirassy. In 2025/26 that produced the league's best defence (34 conceded), 70 goals and 73 points — 13 more than 2024/25 — but the team was still 2nd behind a record-breaking Bayern and collapsed from 2-0 up to lose 3-4 on aggregate to Atalanta in the UCL. The 2026 window replaced experienced attackers (Brandt, Adeyemi) with teenagers (Karetsas 18, Gadou 19, Lerma 18, Prates 17) plus the Nwaneri loan.",
    "principles": [
      "buy-young-develop-sell: 2026 recruits Karetsas, Gadou, Lerma, Prates are all 19 or under",
      "member-owned club with a listed KGaA, run within its own means (no oligarch/state money)",
      "Bundesliga's best defensive record as the platform for a title challenge",
      "coaching stability: Kovač extended to 2027 after finishing 2nd",
      "Südtribüne/Yellow Wall fan culture and 81k attendances as commercial engine"
    ],
    "club_character": "Member-controlled club (BV Borussia 09 e.V. holds the majority of the KGaA, which is listed on the Frankfurt Stock Exchange — Germany's only listed football club). Enormous, loyal fanbase and the 81,365-capacity Signal Iduna Park with the Yellow Wall; media pressure is high but the club is financially self-sustaining and openly positions itself as Bayern's challenger with a fraction of the budget.",
    "ownership": "member-owned (e.V. majority) with a stock-market-listed KGaA (Borussia Dortmund GmbH & Co. KGaA); minority investors include Evonik, Signal Iduna, Puma",
    "political_and_social_context": "Publicly documented: the 2024 sponsorship deal with arms manufacturer Rheinmetall drew organised fan and political criticism; the club has long-running institutional anti-racism/anti-discrimination programmes and Holocaust-remembrance work with supporters; the ultras scene has repeatedly protested against Monday fixtures and DFB commercialisation. No further institution-level positions verified this session.",
    "languages": [
      "de",
      "en"
    ],
    "wage_structure": "hierarchical but below Bayern; wage bill trimmed in 2026 with Brandt, Süle and Özcan off the books (details unverified)",
    "transfer_policy": "Data-led youth acquisition (Karetsas EUR 30m+2m, Gadou ~EUR 19.5m, Prates EUR 12m, Lerma EUR 4m agreed in 2024) funded by selling peak-value or expiring assets (Adeyemi up to EUR 29m, Duranville EUR 5m, Campbell EUR 6m, Wätjen EUR 2.5m); Premier League loan-to-buy (Chukwuemeka EUR ~25m) and loans (Nwaneri).",
    "injury_management_reputation": "Publicly discussed: captain Emre Can tore his ACL in the March 2026 Klassiker and was still extended to 2027; Niklas Süle retired in 2026 after health problems limited his Dortmund career."
